  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A student sits a multiple-choice test. Each question on the test has $n$ possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer to $70%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that they do not know, and the probability that a randomly selected question from the test is answered correctly is $0.76,$ what is the value of $n?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knows the answer. Then ${K}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the student doesn't know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{\u2032})P({K}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $70%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.7,\\,P({K}^{\u2032})=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has $n$ possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{\u2032})=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected question is answered correctly is $0.76,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.76.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{\u2032})P({K}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.76 & =(1)(0.70)+(\\frac{1}{n})(0.3) \\\\ 0.76 & =0.70+\\frac{0.3}{n} \\\\ \\frac{0.3}{n} & =0.06 \\\\ n & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test has $5$ possible answers for each question.",
